From ee9644258c5a92a21a587b26f06a1924fab042c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 20:32:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Content: wiper blade, cabin filter and engine air filter
 guides

Fifteen new guides, taking the catalog from 13 to 28. Every vehicle now
has between three and five premium guides instead of one or two, which is
the difference between a per-vehicle unlock being worth buying and not.

Scope: O2 sensor stays in the covered list per Andy's call; thermostat and
alternator/starter stay out.

WHAT SHIPPED, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY DID NOT

Fifteen of the eighteen possible guides are here. Three are missing on
purpose because their access procedure could not be confirmed:

- Escape cabin air filter. Two sources give incompatible procedures -- one
  says drop the glove box and remove a cover with a Phillips screwdriver,
  the other says it is in the passenger footwell behind a bottom panel with
  a T20 Torx. The 2020+ Escape is a new platform, so the older procedure
  may simply have been carried over wrongly. Not written until confirmed.
- Silverado and Sierra engine air filter. Airbox lid retention could not be
  verified from any credible source. Cold-air-intake install sheets
  describe full airbox removal, which is not the filter-service procedure
  and would be actively misleading if presented as one.

VALIDATOR CHANGE

None of these jobs torque a fastener -- wiper blades snap onto an arm,
filters sit behind clips or one trim screw. The guide console treated a
missing torque spec as publish-blocking, which is right for anything
bolted to the car and wrong for these. RepairGuide gains an optional
noFasteners flag that exempts a guide from that one check and from the
provenance check, since a job with no fasteners has nothing to source.
It is not a way to ship a fastener job without specs: anything that
torques something leaves it unset.

DETAILS WORTH KEEPING

- Civic engine air filter: the 1.5T airbox lid uses four JIS screws with
  8mm hex heads, not a true Phillips, and owners strip them constantly.
  The guide says to use an 8mm socket. The naturally-aspirated 2.0L Civic
  uses snap clips instead, so an LX or Sport video does not match this car
  -- called out explicitly.
- Civic wipers: 26/18 front and NO rear blade. Retailer size charts list a
  14 inch rear for a 2018 Civic because they collapse sedan, coupe and
  hatchback into one entry; that blade is for the hatchback. The guide
  warns against buying it.
- F-150 cabin filter: not a drop-the-glove-box job. Needs a trim panel
  removed, 7mm bolts, and an electrical connector disconnected. Rated
  Moderate rather than Easy for that reason.
- Escape engine air filter: the intake boot clamp has to be loosened and
  the boot pulled back first, unlike the other vehicles. Sourced from the
  Ford owner's manual, the strongest source in the set.

Five new original schematic illustrations in the house style, all
rasterized and visually checked before commit.

Verified: tsc clean; next build compiles clean; all 28 guides pass every
required check; 28 unique ids; all 23 referenced illustrations exist;
free-guide invariant intact; only two recommended gaps remain catalog-wide
(both Jeep provenance, whose OLP call returned nothing above estimated
tier); eslint unchanged at the same 4 pre-existing problems.

